WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.057
Now we move into object-oriented programming.

00:00:03.057 --> 00:00:06.407
A class is a blueprint for creating objects.

00:00:06.407 --> 00:00:12.937
The class describes what kind of data the object should store and what actions the object can perform.

00:00:12.937 --> 00:00:16.842
For example, imagine a CustomerRecord class.

00:00:16.842 --> 00:00:21.207
Each customer record might store a customer ID and a segment.

00:00:21.207 --> 00:00:27.787
The class defines the structure, and each object created from that class stores its own values.

00:00:27.787 --> 00:00:32.902
A useful way to think about this is that a class describes a general type of thing,

00:00:32.902 --> 00:00:36.547
while an object is one specific example of that thing.

00:00:36.547 --> 00:00:39.957
For example, Product could be a class.

00:00:39.957 --> 00:00:42.857
One object might represent a laptop.

00:00:42.857 --> 00:00:45.707
Another object might represent a monitor.

00:00:45.707 --> 00:00:51.107
Both objects follow the same blueprint, but each one stores different values.

00:00:51.107 --> 00:00:55.562
Classes are most useful when data and behavior belong together.

00:00:55.562 --> 00:01:00.212
If you only need one simple calculation, a function may be enough.

00:01:00.212 --> 00:01:06.502
But if you have a concept that stores information and also has actions related to that information,

00:01:06.502 --> 00:01:08.000
a class can be helpful.
